1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,288
This is ECE 358, video 8 a. The topic is reliable data transfer with stop-and-wait. It covers Chapter 3, slides 25 to 31.

2
00:00:12,738 --> 00:00:29,826
After this video, you can name the four problems of a channel and the four mechanisms that fix them. You can draw the stop-and-wait time diagrams. And you can calculate the sender utilization, the throughput, and the efficiency, as in problem set 3.

3
00:00:30,276 --> 00:00:42,671
Start with the problem. Packets travel over an unreliable channel. The channel can do four bad things. First, it can corrupt a packet. A bit flips. This is a bit error.

4
00:00:43,121 --> 00:00:49,051
Second, it can deliver packets out of order. Here packet 2 arrives before packet 1.

5
00:00:49,501 --> 00:00:53,320
Third, it can lose a packet. Packet 3 never arrives.

6
00:00:53,770 --> 00:01:00,746
Fourth, it can deliver the same packet more than once. Packet 4 arrives twice. This is a duplicate.

7
00:01:01,196 --> 00:01:08,769
The slide gives a mnemonic: BOLD. B for bit errors, O for out of order, L for loss, D for duplicates.

8
00:01:09,219 --> 00:01:20,462
How do we get reliable data transfer over this channel? First, the sender needs feedback. The receiver sends an acknowledgement, an ACK, for a packet that it received.

9
00:01:20,912 --> 00:01:32,347
Sequence numbers solve two problems: out-of-order packets and duplicate packets. With a number in each packet, the receiver can put packets in order and find copies.

10
00:01:32,797 --> 00:01:47,666
Timers solve the problem of lost packets. If no ACK comes before the timer expires, the sender retransmits. Protocols that do this are called automatic repeat request protocols, or ARQ.

11
00:01:48,116 --> 00:01:57,737
Error detection codes, such as a checksum, detect bit errors. The receiver discards a bad packet. In this course, this part is implicit.

12
00:01:58,187 --> 00:02:13,142
The simplest ARQ protocol is stop-and-wait. The main idea has three parts. Send a packet. Start a timer. Wait for an ACK. If the timer expires before the ACK comes, retransmit the packet.

13
00:02:13,592 --> 00:02:23,939
The sender sends packet 0 and starts its timer. The receiver gets packet 0 and sends ack 0. The sender gets ack 0 and stops the timer.

14
00:02:24,389 --> 00:02:28,677
Now the sender sends packet 1. The receiver sends ack 1.

15
00:02:29,127 --> 00:02:46,833
Then the sender sends the next packet with sequence number 0 again. Only one packet is in flight at a time. So one bit is enough for the sequence number: 0, 1, 0, 1. This is why stop-and-wait is also called the alternating bit protocol.

16
00:02:47,283 --> 00:02:56,542
Case b: a packet is lost. Packet 0 and ack 0 arrive normally. Then the sender sends packet 1, and the channel loses it.

17
00:02:56,992 --> 00:03:09,877
The receiver does not know about packet 1. So it sends nothing. The sender's timer runs, and no ACK comes. The timer expires. This is a timeout. The sender resends packet 1.

18
00:03:10,327 --> 00:03:19,735
This time packet 1 arrives. The receiver sends ack 1. The sender gets ack 1 and sends packet 0. The timer found the loss.

19
00:03:20,185 --> 00:03:28,292
Case c: an ACK is lost. Packet 1 arrives, and the receiver sends ack 1. But the channel loses ack 1.

20
00:03:28,742 --> 00:03:34,054
The sender does not get an ACK. Its timer expires, and it resends packet 1.

21
00:03:34,504 --> 00:03:46,173
The receiver gets packet 1 again. The sequence number tells it that this is a duplicate. So it discards the packet and does not give it to the app again. It sends ack 1 again.

22
00:03:46,623 --> 00:03:54,773
The sender gets ack 1 and continues with packet 0. Here, the sequence number prevents a duplicate delivery.

23
00:03:55,223 --> 00:04:03,031
Case d: a premature timeout. Packet 1 arrives, and the receiver sends ack 1. But ack 1 is delayed.

24
00:04:03,481 --> 00:04:10,649
The timer expires before ack 1 arrives. The timeout is too early. The sender resends packet 1.

25
00:04:11,099 --> 00:04:23,920
Now ack 1 arrives. The sender sends packet 0. At the same time, the receiver gets the second packet 1. It is a duplicate, so the receiver discards it and sends ack 1 again.

26
00:04:24,370 --> 00:04:41,842
The sender gets this second ack 1. It is a duplicate ACK, so the sender ignores it. The receiver gets packet 0 and sends ack 0. The rule of the slide: any duplicate packet at the receiver, or duplicate ACK at the sender, is discarded.

27
00:04:42,292 --> 00:05:03,092
Stop-and-wait is correct. But is it fast? Here is the example of slide 29. A sender and a receiver use a 1 gigabit per second link, so R is 10 to the power 9 bits per second. The propagation delay is 15 ms. A packet has L equal to 8000 bits.

28
00:05:03,542 --> 00:05:18,603
The time to transmit the packet into the channel is L over R. 8000 bits divided by 10 to the power 9 bits per second is 8 microseconds. That is 0 point 008 ms.

29
00:05:19,053 --> 00:05:28,419
To measure the performance, the slides use the sender utilization, U sender. It is the fraction of time that the sender is busy sending.

30
00:05:28,869 --> 00:05:40,602
Here is the time diagram of slide 30. It is not to scale. The sender transmits the first bit at time 0. It transmits the last bit at time L over R.

31
00:05:41,052 --> 00:05:56,775
The bits travel for the propagation delay. The first bit arrives after d prop. The last bit arrives at L over R plus d prop. Then the receiver sends the ACK. The slides take the ACK transmission time as zero.

32
00:05:57,225 --> 00:06:08,595
The ACK travels back for another d prop. It arrives at the sender at RTT plus L over R. RTT is the round-trip time, 2 times d prop.

33
00:06:09,045 --> 00:06:22,315
Only then can the sender send the next packet. So in each cycle of RTT plus L over R, the sender is busy for only L over R. For the rest of the cycle, it is idle.

34
00:06:22,765 --> 00:06:29,997
From the diagram, U sender equals L over R, divided by RTT plus L over R.

35
00:06:30,447 --> 00:06:54,383
Put in the numbers. L over R is 0 point 008 ms. RTT is 2 times 15, which is 30 ms. So U sender is 0 point 008 divided by 30 point 008. That is 0 point 0 0 0 2 7, or 0 point 0 2 7 percent.

36
00:06:54,833 --> 00:07:10,043
The bar shows one cycle to scale. The blue part, where the sender is busy, is too thin to see. It is 1 part in 3751 of the cycle. The sender waits for the ACK almost all the time.

37
00:07:10,493 --> 00:07:35,261
The throughput is the utilization times the link speed. 0 point 0 0 0 2 7 times 10 to the power 9 is 270 kilobits per second, over a 1 gigabit per second link. With the exact value of U, it is 266 point 6 kilobits per second. The protocol limits the performance of the link.

38
00:07:35,711 --> 00:07:41,727
Now the question bank. Here are questions 19 and 20. Pause the video and try them.

39
00:07:42,177 --> 00:07:54,252
Question 19. The channel can corrupt packets with bit errors. It can deliver them out of order. It can lose them. And it can deliver duplicates. B, O, L, D.

40
00:07:54,702 --> 00:08:04,750
Question 20. The four mechanisms are: error detection, such as a checksum. Acknowledgements from the receiver. Sequence numbers. And timers.

41
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:16,272
Question 21. Pause the video and try it. Here is a good answer: draw the packet loss case. The sender sends a packet, starts a timer, and waits for the ACK.

42
00:08:16,722 --> 00:08:26,407
Mechanism 1 is the sequence number. The packets carry 0, 1, 0, and so on. The receiver uses it to find duplicates.

43
00:08:26,857 --> 00:08:35,327
Mechanism 2 is the ACK. Ack 0 and ack 1 tell the sender that the packet arrived. Then it sends the next packet.

44
00:08:35,777 --> 00:08:44,694
Mechanism 3 is the timer. Packet 1 is lost, so no ACK comes. At the timeout, the sender retransmits packet 1.

45
00:08:45,144 --> 00:08:55,448
Mechanism 4 is the checksum. The receiver checks each packet. It discards a packet with bit errors, so the sender's timer handles it like a loss.

46
00:08:55,898 --> 00:09:01,146
Questions 22, 23 and 24. Pause the video and try them.

47
00:09:01,596 --> 00:09:14,887
Question 22. ARQ means automatic repeat request. The sender retransmits a packet automatically when no ACK arrives before the timeout. The receiver does not need to ask.

48
00:09:15,337 --> 00:09:30,206
Question 23. The main drawback is low sender utilization. The sender is idle while it waits for each ACK. In the slide example, it is busy only 0 point 0 2 7 percent of the time.

49
00:09:30,656 --> 00:09:40,107
Question 24. One packet. Stop-and-wait has only one unACKed packet, so a timeout resends only that packet.

50
00:09:40,557 --> 00:09:44,930
Questions 25 and 26. Pause the video and try them.

51
00:09:45,380 --> 00:10:02,852
Question 25. The receiver sends a duplicate ACK when a duplicate packet arrives. This happens after an ACK loss, as here, or after a premature timeout, when the ACK is only delayed. The receiver discards the copy and sends the ACK again.

52
00:10:03,302 --> 00:10:14,075
Question 26. The metric is the sender utilization, U sender: the fraction of time the sender is busy sending. The throughput is U times R.

53
00:10:14,525 --> 00:10:22,717
Now problem set 3. Problem 1. Stop-and-go is another name for stop-and-wait. Pause the video and try it.

54
00:10:23,167 --> 00:10:37,823
Use the solution's symbols. d is the time to transmit a data frame, L over C. P is the propagation delay. The data frame is 840 bytes, which is 6720 bits.

55
00:10:38,273 --> 00:10:47,127
a is the time to transmit the ACK frame. The ACK is 40 bytes, which is 320 bits. It also travels for P.

56
00:10:47,577 --> 00:11:02,873
So one cycle takes T equals d plus 2 P plus a. The throughput is 1 over T packets per second. The efficiency is d over T. This is U sender, but this time the ACK transmission time is included.

57
00:11:03,323 --> 00:11:30,907
At 1 kilobit per second, d is 6720 over 1000, which is 6 point 7 2 seconds. a is 0 point 3 2 seconds. T is 6 point 7 2 plus 0 point 0 1 6 plus 0 point 3 2, which is 7 point 0 5 6 seconds. The throughput is 0 point 1 4 2 packets per second. The efficiency is 95 point 2 percent.

58
00:11:31,357 --> 00:11:50,578
At 100 kilobits per second, d is 67 point 2 ms and a is 3 point 2 ms. T is 86 point 4 ms. The throughput is 11 point 6 packets per second, and the efficiency is 77 point 8 percent.

59
00:11:51,028 --> 00:12:12,617
At 10 megabits per second, d is 0 point 6 7 2 ms and a is 0 point 0 3 2 ms. T is 16 point 7 0 4 ms. The throughput is 59 point 9 packets per second, and the efficiency is 4 point 0 2 percent.

60
00:12:13,067 --> 00:12:29,601
At 1 gigabit per second, d is 6 point 7 2 microseconds. T is about 16 ms. The throughput is 62 point 5 packets per second, and the efficiency is 0 point 0 4 2 percent.

61
00:12:30,051 --> 00:12:50,147
The bars show why. At a high rate, d becomes very small. But the two propagation delays, 16 ms, do not change. So the sender waits almost all the time. The throughput cannot go above 1 over 16 ms, 62 point 5 packets per second.

62
00:12:50,597 --> 00:12:58,533
Problem 2. ABP is the alternating bit protocol, so stop-and-wait again. Pause the video and try it.

63
00:12:58,983 --> 00:13:09,265
The propagation delay P is 5 kilometers divided by 2 times 10 to the power 8 meters per second. That is 25 microseconds.

64
00:13:09,715 --> 00:13:25,417
The packet and the ACK are both 1500 bits. So d and a are both 1500 divided by 10 to the power 7. That is 150 microseconds. This diagram is to scale.

65
00:13:25,867 --> 00:13:41,611
T is 150 plus 2 times 25 plus 150, which is 350 microseconds. So the sender transmits 1 over T, about 2857 packets per second.

66
00:13:42,061 --> 00:13:50,999
Problem 2 b. Pause the video and try it. Five identical links are connected in series. There is a buffer at each connection.

67
00:13:51,449 --> 00:14:11,972
Each link runs its own alternating bit protocol. When a packet arrives at a buffer, it can go on at once on the next link. So every link carries one packet per T at the same time. End to end, the rate is still 1 over T, about 2857 packets per second.

68
00:14:12,422 --> 00:14:38,065
Now a packet or its ACK is corrupted with probability p on each link. The solution assumes that each try takes T and succeeds with probability 1 minus p. So the rate is 1 minus p, over T. For p equal to 0 point 0 1, that is about 2829 packets per second. The solution writes about 2828.

69
00:14:38,515 --> 00:14:59,101
Compare one 25-kilometer link. P is 125 microseconds. T is 150 plus 2 times 125 plus 150, which is 550 microseconds. The rate is about 1818 packets per second.

70
00:14:59,551 --> 00:15:12,053
So 5 short links in series give a higher rate than one long link. On a short link, the sender waits only 2 P, 50 microseconds, for each ACK, not 250.

71
00:15:12,503 --> 00:15:24,919
Here is the recap. One. The channel can cause bit errors, out-of-order delivery, loss, and duplicates. The mechanisms are the checksum, the ACK, the sequence number, and the timer.

72
00:15:25,369 --> 00:15:36,569
Two. Stop-and-wait sends one packet, starts a timer, and waits for the ACK. On a timeout, it resends. The sequence number alternates between 0 and 1.

73
00:15:37,019 --> 00:15:48,432
Three. A duplicate packet comes after an ACK loss or a premature timeout. The receiver discards it and sends the ACK again. The sender ignores a duplicate ACK.

74
00:15:48,882 --> 00:16:00,445
Four. U sender is L over R, divided by RTT plus L over R. It is very small when RTT is large compared with L over R.

75
00:16:00,895 --> 00:16:16,489
Five. In the problem set form, T is d plus 2 P plus a. The throughput is 1 over T. The efficiency is d over T. The next video covers pipelining and Go-Back-N. That is the end of video 8 a.
